timestamp=2026-11-04T19:17:55.901Z level=INFO run=b8e4cffa message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python << 'PY'\n# ST060 has corr ~0.444 with district median, amp_ratio ~1.37 - not terrible but not great. \n# Its residuals have |corr| ~0.66 with rain_accumulation_mm. That means deviations from local peers align strongly with rain.\n# Is this considered \"ordinary in aggregate\" but deviations too neatly lined up with an environmental driver?\n# Compare to others: ST040 has very high amp_ratio (2.86) and high residual-weather corr - that’s not “ordinary in aggregate”.\n# ST023 also has high residual-rain corr but let’s look at its overall behavior.\n# ST039 high residual-wind corr, but also high corr with peers (0.73) - not the case.\n# ST058: low corr with peers, high lag improvement (we already saw rhythm issue? ST058 appeared in rhythm candidates with lag improvement). Also high wind corr to residuals.\n# So multiple candidates have strong residual-weather correlation. But which one is “ordinary in aggregate”?\n# “ordinary in aggregate” could mean basic summary stats look fine (mean, variance similar) and maybe corr with peers is reasonable? Or just not obviously broken.\n# Let’s look at ST060 more closely. Also check if it has many NaNs as we saw earlier - ST060 had 9 NaNs (most). That’s another red flag. Does that count as “anomalous behavior”? But we need to pick 3 different stations exhibiting different types of anomalous behavior.\n# Also the hints: \"Some measurement rows are incomplete, so treat short gaps carefully and prefer methods that preserve local time-series structure rather than turning missingness itself into the main signal.\"\n# So missingness shouldn’t be the main signal for choosing a faulty station. But ST060 has gaps. That might be part of the dataset’s messiness, not necessarily the “faulty behavior” type the question is asking us to identify by pattern.\n# Let’s look at all 3 cases again.\n\n# Case 1: \"One problematic station broadly follows local patterns, but not with the same rhythm as its peers.\"\n# Best match: ST020 (PM10, PSIE_POLE). corr at lag 0 is only 0.16, but best_corr is ~0.59 at lag +1. Amplitude is similar. That’s classic phase/timing shift - rhythm different. Also appears in rhythm candidates.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
