timestamp=2026-11-04T19:18:05.321Z level=INFO run=b8e4cffa message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python << 'PY'\n# Case 2: \"Another looks plausible point-by-point yet its swings are unusually muted compared with comparable local stations.\"\n# Best match: ST051 (PM25, FABRYCZNA). amp_ratio ~0.82 (low), corr ~0.63 (reasonable). Compared to peers, std much smaller. Point-by-point plausible but muted swings.\n\n# Case 3: \"A third appears ordinary in aggregate, but its remaining deviations line up a bit too neatly with one environmental driver.\"\n# “Ordinary in aggregate” - maybe decent corr with district median and amp_ratio near 1? Let’s find which station fits that.\n# ST023: corr_st ~0.60, amp_ratio ~1.48 - not “ordinary” in being typical; swings are amplified. Also high residual-rain corr.\n# ST039: high corr with peers (0.73), amp_ratio ~1.53, also high residual-wind corr - not muted and not average.\n# ST058: we saw it has rhythm issue (lag improvement) and also muted amp_ratio 0.94 but low corr. Also appears in multiple categories.\n# ST012: corr_st ~0.71, amp_ratio ~1.77 - amplified, not ordinary.\n# ST019: similar, amplified.\n# ST060: corr_st ~0.44 (lowest among peers in some sense), amp_ratio ~1.37 - not strikingly “ordinary”. Also has lots of NaNs.\n# Another one: ST099 has high corr_st (0.79) and amp_ratio ~1.37 - quite ordinary in aggregate shape? And residual-temp corr is -0.526 (moderate). Or ST005? Let’s look up more.\nimport pandas as pd\n\nstats = pd.read_csv('/tmp/candidate_stats.csv')\n# Find stations with corr_st reasonably high (0.6-0.8) and amp_ratio near 1 (0.9-1.1) - “ordinary in aggregate”\ncand3 = stats[(stats['corr'] > 0.6) & (stats['corr'] < 0.8) & (stats['amp_ratio'] > 0.9) & (stats['amp_ratio'] < 1.1)]\nprint(cand3.to_string())\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
